WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:04.580
Every year, reporters and editors at Science choose

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.080
nine runners up and one breakthrough of the year--

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.340
they look for the most significant scientific discoveries,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.340
developments and trends.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.160
But before we get to the breakthrough,

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.540
here are the runners up

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.920
In a detector beneath the South Pole, a ghostly particle

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.200
called a neutrino left a telltale track

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.460
researchers traced it back to its likely source:

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.460
a bright galaxy called a blazar, far away.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.840
The finding suggests blazars may also be the source of

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.320
mysterious ultra–high-energy cosmic rays.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.960
Deep beneath the Greenland ice sheet,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.460
scientists have discovered a massive crater,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.100
it was created by the impact

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.600
of a mountain sized asteroid

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.480
The impact is relatively recent

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.740
and hasn't yet been firmly dated,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.960
but the event may help explain some

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.600
puzzling climate changes.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.480
This ancient fossil is more than

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.480
half a billion years old.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.480
But was it an animal?

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.220
An intriguing find suggest it was.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.940
Scientists discovered traces of cholesterol,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.060
a signature of animal life, associated with these fossils

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.120
The feat indicates the fossils,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.920
which belong to a mysterious class called Ediacara

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.020
were some of Earth’s earliest animals

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.980
The first drug based on a gene-silencing reaction

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:36.780
called RNA interference (RNAi),

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.100
won approval this year for

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.920
use in the United States and Europe.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.920
Researchers discovered RNAi,

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.560
which blocks messenger RNA, 20 years ago

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.020
The drug, which targets a rare hereditary disease,

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.700
could be the first of many.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.580
Until recently, sexual harassment in science

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.900
has been underreported and largely ignored

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.479
But 2018 brought signs of change.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.240
Several institutions upheld allegations against

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.480
prominent scientists accused of sexual harassment,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.440
discrimination, and  bullying,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.520
and the U.S. National Academies of Science

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.400
issued a report calling for

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.080
systemic changes to prevent such abuse.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.240
This year saw increasing evidence that cellular

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.340
components condense into tiny droplets--

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.840
a process called phase separation.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.720
The droplets can perform critical functions.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.340
Researchers have found they play an unexpected role

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.360
in the transcription of genes,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.420
And they identified a possible path

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.780
to dissolving solidified droplets

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.040
that clog the brain

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.420
and contribute to diseases such as

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.000
amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.980
From a cave in Siberia, researchers recovered

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.400
DNA contained in a bone fragment

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.740
of a woman who lived more than 50,000 years ago

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.620
Analysis indicated her mother was a Neanderthal

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.840
and her father a Denisovan,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.780
a mysterious group of archaic humans.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.860
The finding is the most direct evidence yet that

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.120
genetically distinct types of ancient humans interbred.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.180
Police detectives have used a new tool

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.180
forensic geneaology

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.560
to crack about 20 cold cases, including crimes

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.100
committed by the suspected Golden State Killer

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:34.580
The criminals left DNA at the crime scenes

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.320
The detectives searched through

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.320
a public DNA database

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.060
to identify people who had similar DNA

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.080
and were likely to be related to the criminals

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.560
Researchers have developed a technique based

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.620
on electron microscopy that allows them

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.620
that allows them to determine the precise shape

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.180
of small organic molecules

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.960
The new approach is simple, fast,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.320
and works on tiny samples.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.000
It is likely to help speed up drug discovery.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.860
And now, for the breakthrough of the year:

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:12.000
From at least the time of Hippocrates, biologists

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.740
have been transfixed by a mystery --

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.960
how does a single cell develop

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.570
into an adult animal

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.880
with multiple organs and billions of cells.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.400
Now a combination of technologies

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.780
is allowing scientists to track the development

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:30.180
of organisms and organs in stunning detail

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.960
cell by cell, through time.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.540
They can see when genes in individual cells switch on,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.720
cueing the cells to play their specialized parts.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.900
The techniques are like having the sheet music

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.560
used by an orchestra

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.860
that indicates when the strings, the brass,

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.600
percussion, and woodwinds should

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.740
chime in to create a symphony

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.520
These groundbreaking studies have already

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.760
mapped cell fates in organisms from zebrafish to mice,

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.600
and more is sure to come.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.840
Science is recognizing this combination

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.800
of technologies, and its potential for spurring advances

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.800
in basic research and medicine,

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.880
as the 2018 Breakthrough of the Year.

